import { FixedSizeGrid } from "react-window";

// Cell renderer: must apply the style prop to the outermost element
const Cell = ({ columnIndex, rowIndex, style, data }: any) => {
  const row = data[rowIndex];
  const value = row[columnIndex];
  return (
    <div style={style} className="border-b border-r flex items-center px-2 text-sm truncate">
      {value}
    </div>
  );
};

function VirtualizedTable({ rows, columns }: { rows: string[][]; columns: string[] }) {
  return (
    <FixedSizeGrid
      columnCount={columns.length}      // 15
      columnWidth={150}                 // Fixed column width
      rowCount={rows.length}            // 5000
      rowHeight={40}                    // Fixed row height
      height={600}                      // Visible height of the container
      width={1200}                      // Visible width of the container
      itemData={rows}                   // Avoid recreating closure references
    >
      {Cell}
    </FixedSizeGrid>
  );
}

full_response:
The root cause of the 4-5 second freeze is not the data volume itself, but the fact that the browser is trying to create **75,000 DOM nodes** simultaneously (5000 rows × 15 columns). The solution is **row virtualization**: only render the visible rows in the viewport (typically 20-30 rows), keeping the DOM node count constant regardless of data size.

### Code Example: Virtualizing a 5000-Row Table with `react-window`

`FixedSizeGrid` supports simultaneous row and column virtualization, suitable for your 15-column scenario. The key is to pass the `style` prop to each cell element—this is the positioning signal from `react-window` to the cell.

```tsx
import { FixedSizeGrid } from "react-window";

// Cell renderer: must apply the style prop to the outermost element
const Cell = ({ columnIndex, rowIndex, style, data }: any) => {
  const row = data[rowIndex];
  const value = row[columnIndex];
  return (
    <div style={style} className="border-b border-r flex items-center px-2 text-sm truncate">
      {value}
    </div>
  );
};

function VirtualizedTable({ rows, columns }: { rows: string[][]; columns: string[] }) {
  return (
    <FixedSizeGrid
      columnCount={columns.length}      // 15
      columnWidth={150}                 // Fixed column width
      rowCount={rows.length}            // 5000
      rowHeight={40}                    // Fixed row height
      height={600}                      // Visible height of the container
      width={1200}                      // Visible width of the container
      itemData={rows}                   // Avoid recreating closure references
    >
      {Cell}
    </FixedSizeGrid>
  );
}
```

**Why this approach works**: When `react-window` initializes, it only renders the rows visible in the viewport plus a small buffer zone (typically 20-40 rows). During scrolling, it dynamically replaces the rendered content based on the scroll position—the DOM node count never exceeds a few hundred, and the main thread is no longer blocked by tens of thousands of nodes. For tables with fixed row heights, this is the lowest-cost, immediately effective solution.

### Architectural Roadmap: From Freeze to Smoothness

**Step 1: Confirm the rendering bottleneck (do this first)**

Before modifying code, record a performance trace using the Chrome DevTools Performance panel. You'll see that the "Recalculate Style" and "Layout" phases during page load take several seconds. This confirms that the bottleneck is **rendering**, not data processing or network requests. If data processing itself (such as sorting and filtering 5000 rows) also blocks the main thread, it needs to be moved to a Web Worker or performed on the server side.

**Step 2: Choose the virtualization granularity**

Your scenario (5000 rows × 15 columns) is not large enough to require column virtualization, because 15 columns won't cause rendering pressure. But if the column count grows to 30+ in the future, or if a large number of cells contain complex renderers (such as avatars, progress bars, action buttons), `FixedSizeGrid`'s bidirectional virtualization becomes necessary. For now, starting with **row virtualization** alone is sufficient—you can use `FixedSizeList` (if you don't need horizontal scrolling) or keep `FixedSizeGrid` for smoother future scaling.

**Step 3: Sticky headers and column alignment**

`react-window` doesn't automatically handle table headers. You need to place the header row outside the virtualized scroll container, using CSS `position: sticky; top: 0` to keep it fixed. Ensure that the width settings for header cells and body cells are exactly identical (both using `columnWidth={150}` or CSS Grid), otherwise horizontal scrolling will cause misalignment.

**Step 4: Debounce rendering of dynamic row heights**

If some cells have variable content heights (such as multi-line text or expandable rows), `FixedSizeGrid`'s fixed height assumption will break. At this point, switch to `VariableSizeGrid` and use `ResizeObserver` to measure actual row heights. Note: Dynamic measurement has a performance cost and is only worth the complexity when heights truly cannot be predicted. For most admin dashboards, truncating text with ellipsis and fixing row heights is the more pragmatic choice.

**Step 5: Server-side pagination for data beyond 10k rows**

Current data volume is 5000 rows, and client-side virtualization is completely sufficient. However, if the table will grow to tens of thousands of rows in the future, relying solely on client-side virtualization will still have problems: all data needs to be loaded into memory first, initial load time will lengthen, and browser scrollbar height may hit the upper limit (approximately 22 million pixels). At that point, switch to a **server-side pagination + row virtualization** combination: each page returns 100-200 rows, and within the page, use virtualization to ensure smooth scrolling. This pattern decouples "data volume" from "rendering pressure," which is the correct architecture for large-scale dashboards.

